WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.608
Bianca works in finance, and she automated reading the invoices her suppliers send.

00:00:06.908 --> 00:00:12.509
Every company in Brazil is identified by a tax ID, fourteen digits with two check

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:13.754
digits at the end.

00:00:14.404 --> 00:00:19.909
Her prompt asked: return the issuer's tax ID, the issue date and the total amount,

00:00:19.909 --> 00:00:21.185
as structured data.

00:00:21.485 --> 00:00:24.345
In the first week, it worked on almost everything.

00:00:24.995 --> 00:00:26.740
Then the odd cases started.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.231
One reply opened with a friendly sentence: sure, here's the data.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:36.036
Another returned the total as text, with the currency symbol, when the system

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:37.089
expected a number.

00:00:37.389 --> 00:00:40.166
A third gave the field a different name.

00:00:40.816 --> 00:00:43.380
Each case became one more patch in the code.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.531
The parser grew larger than the business logic, and it still failed a few times a

00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:48.770
day.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.959
In this lesson, you'll see how to get the model to deliver data your code can use

00:00:53.959 --> 00:00:54.985
without guessing.

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:57.156
And why that's only half of the job.

00:00:58.356 --> 00:01:00.860
The fix starts with a change of mindset.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.666
Stop asking for a format in the prompt text, and start declaring the format as part

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:07.462
of the call.

00:01:08.112 --> 00:01:13.674
Current APIs accept a schema, a description of the fields, their types and their

00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:14.717
allowed values.

00:01:15.017 --> 00:01:18.513
And they constrain generation so the output follows it.

00:01:18.813 --> 00:01:23.613
It's the difference between handing someone a blank sheet of paper, and handing them

00:01:23.613 --> 00:01:25.156
a form with labeled fields.

00:01:25.806 --> 00:01:27.846
But here's the part people miss.

00:01:28.146 --> 00:01:30.633
The schema guarantees shape, not truth.

00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:35.114
A tax ID can have fourteen digits and still be invalid.

00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:39.709
So the second half of the pattern is checking business rules in code.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:43.626
Check digits, a date in the past, a positive amount.

00:01:43.926 --> 00:01:47.612
Shape guaranteed by the API, content checked by you.

00:01:48.812 --> 00:01:50.436
Let's follow one invoice.

00:01:50.736 --> 00:01:53.786
The input is the text that needs to become data.

00:01:54.436 --> 00:01:55.968
The call carries the schema.

00:01:56.268 --> 00:01:59.468
The model can only generate outputs in that shape.

00:02:00.118 --> 00:02:03.923
The SDK turns the response into a typed object.

00:02:04.223 --> 00:02:07.690
No regular expressions, no hunting for braces.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.749
Then your code checks the rules the schema can't express.

00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:15.926
Valid data goes straight into the accounting system.

00:02:16.576 --> 00:02:21.775
And data with the right shape, but suspicious content, goes to a person for review.

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.067
Here's a real invoice from a bakery.

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:31.737
A tax ID, an issue date in August, a total of one thousand two hundred thirty-four

00:02:31.737 --> 00:02:35.387
dollars, and two items, bread rolls and coffee.

00:02:36.037 --> 00:02:41.659
With return the data in the prompt, a realistic reply had three shape failures at

00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:42.006
once.

00:02:42.306 --> 00:02:48.261
Text around the data, the wrong field name, and the total as text with a currency

00:02:48.261 --> 00:02:48.776
symbol.

00:02:49.076 --> 00:02:51.646
Bianca's parser needed three fixes.

00:02:51.946 --> 00:02:53.793
The next week, a fourth showed up.

00:02:54.443 --> 00:02:59.310
With a declared schema, the API can only emit four fields.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:05.091
The issuer's tax ID, the issue date, the total as a number, and the list of items.

00:03:05.391 --> 00:03:08.045
The SDK hands back an object.

00:03:08.695 --> 00:03:12.024
And then Bianca's code still has work to do.

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:15.300
The tax ID has to pass the check digit rule.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.286
The items have to add up to the total.

00:03:18.936 --> 00:03:21.147
And the issue date can't be in the future.

00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:26.243
If any of these fail, the invoice goes to review, with the list of what failed.

00:03:27.443 --> 00:03:28.916
Here's one for you.

00:03:29.216 --> 00:03:32.489
The model returned an object exactly in the schema.

00:03:32.789 --> 00:03:35.218
The tax ID has fourteen digits.

00:03:35.518 --> 00:03:37.390
But the check digits don't match.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.479
What does the pattern say to do?

00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:46.940
Validate the check digits in code, and send it to human review when they fail.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:48.557
Shape isn't truth.

00:03:48.857 --> 00:03:54.970
An invalid fourteen digit value passes a text schema and breaks the issuer's record.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.965
And asking again, over and over, until it passes?

00:03:59.265 --> 00:04:03.209
A retry on the same document usually returns the same misread.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:09.143
The review queue exists exactly for the case where the source is unreadable, or the

00:04:09.143 --> 00:04:10.297
model misread it.

00:04:11.497 --> 00:04:13.658
Structured output isn't for everything.

00:04:13.958 --> 00:04:19.242
If the output is text for a person to read, like a chat reply, forcing a schema only

00:04:19.242 --> 00:04:20.563
stiffens the writing.

00:04:20.863 --> 00:04:22.217
Nobody's going to parse it.

00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:27.419
If it's a short free text the code only displays, same thing.

00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:33.881
And if your schema would be one field called answer, of type text, it guarantees

00:04:33.881 --> 00:04:34.462
nothing.

00:04:34.762 --> 00:04:36.355
It just adds ceremony.

00:04:37.005 --> 00:04:38.807
Now the most common mistake.

00:04:39.107 --> 00:04:41.626
Every invoice has an issuer.

00:04:41.926 --> 00:04:44.412
But not every service contract has a penalty.

00:04:44.712 --> 00:04:49.284
If the penalty field is required, the model invents something like ten percent

00:04:49.284 --> 00:04:51.980
standard, just so it doesn't break the schema.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:56.953
So mark as optional whatever can really be missing.

00:04:57.253 --> 00:04:58.219
And measure it.

00:04:58.519 --> 00:05:03.867
In your eval, include documents where the field doesn't exist, and count how often

00:05:03.867 --> 00:05:07.324
the model returns empty, versus how often it invents.

00:05:07.624 --> 00:05:10.982
And prefer a closed list of values over open text.

00:05:11.282 --> 00:05:15.299
An open text field is how super urgent ends up in your priority column.

00:05:16.499 --> 00:05:20.183
With a native schema, the output is almost always valid.

00:05:20.483 --> 00:05:22.921
So valid stops being a metric.

00:05:23.221 --> 00:05:24.893
It hides content errors.

00:05:25.543 --> 00:05:30.009
Evaluate field by field, against labeled values.

00:05:30.309 --> 00:05:31.709
And normalize first.

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:37.136
The invoice says one thousand two hundred thirty-four dollars with a comma, and the

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:38.557
label holds the number.

00:05:38.857 --> 00:05:44.146
Compare text to text, and the eval punishes formatting while a wrong tax ID with the

00:05:44.146 --> 00:05:46.160
right punctuation slips through.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:52.194
Measure the invention rate on documents where the field truly doesn't exist.

00:05:52.844 --> 00:05:57.016
And track what goes to human review, and how much of it was a real error.

00:05:57.316 --> 00:06:02.958
In production, a validation rule that suddenly fires a lot usually means a supplier

00:06:02.958 --> 00:06:04.386
changed their layout.

00:06:05.036 --> 00:06:12.025
Version the schema the way you version the prompt, and record both on every trace.

00:06:12.325 --> 00:06:16.696
A new required field pushes invention on old documents.

00:06:16.996 --> 00:06:21.058
A removed field breaks code that still reads the old name.

00:06:21.708 --> 00:06:23.380
And one security point.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.559
Structured output removes one class of risk.

00:06:26.859 --> 00:06:30.928
Your code stops interpreting free text that could contain anything.

00:06:31.228 --> 00:06:36.226
But the field values still come from a model that read an outside document.

00:06:36.526 --> 00:06:38.746
Treat them as untrusted input.

00:06:39.046 --> 00:06:43.023
A description field copied into a web page can carry a script.

00:06:43.323 --> 00:06:47.267
A field pasted into a database query is an injection.

00:06:47.567 --> 00:06:52.356
Escape before displaying, use parameters in queries, and never execute a field as

00:06:52.356 --> 00:06:52.652
code.

00:06:53.852 --> 00:06:57.385
On the page, the same extraction comes in four versions.

00:06:57.685 --> 00:07:03.158
They all start the same way: one call, with the schema attached, that returns a

00:07:03.158 --> 00:07:04.059
typed object.

00:07:04.709 --> 00:07:06.528
Then three checks in code.

00:07:06.828 --> 00:07:09.588
The check digits, the date, and the sum.

00:07:09.888 --> 00:07:12.159
Anything that fails goes to review.

00:07:12.459 --> 00:07:14.300
Everything else is saved.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:19.230
What changes between versions is the provider and the type system.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:23.369
In Python, one class defines both the schema and the type.

00:07:23.669 --> 00:07:29.049
In TypeScript, a Zod object does the same, and the compiler knows every field.

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:34.240
There's a version on OpenAI's SDK with the very same schema.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:39.415
And LangChain wraps both providers, so the business checks don't change when the

00:07:39.415 --> 00:07:40.085
model does.

00:07:41.285 --> 00:07:43.084
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:43.384 --> 00:07:45.360
Don't ask the model for a format.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:48.672
Declare it, and let the API hold the shape.

00:07:48.972 --> 00:07:54.295
Then check the content in code, because a perfect form can still be filled in wrong.

00:07:54.945 --> 00:07:59.081
In the next lesson, we'll use this same idea in the other direction: the model

00:07:59.081 --> 00:08:01.945
filling in a form that asks your code to do something.

00:08:02.245 --> 00:08:04.159
Tools and function calling.

00:08:04.459 --> 00:08:05.289
See you there.
